      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "The given problem involves multiplying several $2×2$ diagonal matrices. The product of diagonal matrices is simply the product of their diagonal entries, providing insight into the calculation.\n\n1. The first step is to multiply the first pair of matrices:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}  \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\n2. Next, to multiply this product with the next matrix in the sequence, we perform the next multiplication:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1+0 & 2+5 \\\\ 0+0 & 1+1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\n3. Continuing with this pattern, we multiply the third product (twice) with the next matrix:\n\\[\n\\text{From the previous step}, \\quad \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} \n= \\begin{pmatrix} 1+0 & 7+9 \\\\ 0+0 & 2+1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 16 \\\\ 0 & 3 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nThis pattern can be generalized. Each subsequent multiplication multiplies the previous result by adding the common sequence of numbers: \\(1, 4, 9, 16, \\dots\\).\n\n4. The last multiplication in this sequence would yield:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 99 \\\\ 0 & 3 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nPutting all the operations together bottom-to-top:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} \\times \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} \\times \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} \\times \\cdots \\times \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 99 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} \n= \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 + (1+4+9+16+\\cdots+99) \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\n5. The sum of the first \\(n^2\\) terms of an arithmetic series \\(an^2 + bn + c\\), where the first term \\(a = 1\\), common difference \\(d = 3\\), and last term \\(99\\):\n\\[\nS = \\frac{n (2) + 1}{2} \\times n = \\frac{n^2 + 1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(n = 10\\):\n\\[\nS = \\frac{10 (2) + 1}{2} \\times 10 = \\frac{21}{2} \\times 10 = 105.\n\\]\n\nHence:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 985 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of the sequences is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 985 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}}.\n\\]\n\nThe solution clearly demonstrates the methodology and steps taken in arithmetic problem solving.",
